Z||000000||DISenchanted||10-26-1999||07:22 PM||||I need a little help, and my Hayes Manual is MIA.  When I bought the car, the ebrake lever was shattered.  I got a new lever from TOM (isn't he great) and I've started to put it on.  Now I'm stuck, how do I releive enough tension on the cable to hook it to the new handle?<P>One other question, bleeding the fuel line for the purpose of replacing the filter?  How do I accomplish that?||||reg||1
Z||000001||kornholio||10-26-1999||07:55 PM||||I was just about to ask an E-brake question, but since you have the topic, I'll put it here... I got a new E-brake cable when my car was certified and when I dis-engage the E-brake, it doesn't release all the way.  I have to get behind the car and pull the cable myself.  Does anyone know if this is an easy fix or should I leave it to the mechanic?||||reg||1
Z||000002||RZURCTN||10-26-1999||10:39 PM||||Since I just ripped mine all apart and have been lubing a stuck cable, I might be able to help.  The tension for the Park Brake lever is provided by a spring and control arm at the brake calipers.  If your  Park Brake has been inop. for a while it might be stuck at the caliper(mine did this). I worked the lever back and forth for a while and it loosened up.  To adjust tension on the cable there is an adjuster where all the cables meet up just below the trunk.  ||||reg||1
